import{_ as t,c as o,o as s,ah as r}from"./chunks/framework.RCw5T9E9.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Export","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Export"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"logs/export.md","filePath":"logs/export.md"}'),n={name:"logs/export.md"};function a(i,e,l,h,g,d){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="export" tabindex="-1">Export <a class="header-anchor" href="#export" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Export&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Events from the <strong>Logs</strong> tab can be saved to an <strong>HTML</strong> report and opened in any browser. This is useful for bug reports, for sharing a scenario with colleagues and for analysis outside the page.</p><p>General information about the tab: <a href="./general.html">Working with logs</a>.</p><hr><h2 id="export-format" tabindex="-1">Export format <a class="header-anchor" href="#export-format" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Export format&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every export is one <strong>HTML</strong> file: a static report that opens in the browser. There is no separate JSON export.</p><p>The data inside the report is compact, like the live log (see <a href="./general.html#how-events-are-stored">How events are stored</a>): store changes are diffs, a request and its response share one network entry, equal bodies are stored once, and identical values in any event are stored once as a shared snapshot. Titles, Before / After states, snapshot values and request / response details are restored when the report is opened.</p><p>The embedded data uses format version 1.</p><hr><h2 id="what-is-exported" tabindex="-1">What is exported <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-exported" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is exported&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="export-logs" tabindex="-1">Export Logs <a class="header-anchor" href="#export-logs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Export Logs&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Without a selection — all events visible in the table after <strong>Search</strong> and <strong>Type</strong> filters.</li><li>With a selection — only the rows ticked in the checkbox column; the button shows <strong>Export Logs (n)</strong>.</li><li>Network events are always exported as a pair: when only <strong>Send request</strong> or only <strong>Receive response</strong> of a request is filtered or selected, the other event is added to the file too. A WebSocket frame stays the event that was selected.</li></ul><p>The button is disabled while the filtered list is empty.</p><h3 id="single-event" tabindex="-1">Single event <a class="header-anchor" href="#single-event" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Single event&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Export event</strong> in the <strong>⋮</strong> menu of the details header or in the row context menu exports one event.</p><p>For network events the item is called <strong>Export request &amp; response</strong>: the <strong>Send request</strong> and <strong>Receive response</strong> events of the same request are exported together in one file, so the request and its response are never separated. A WebSocket event is exported on its own.</p><h3 id="pinia-changes-in-a-partial-export" tabindex="-1">Pinia changes in a partial export <a class="header-anchor" href="#pinia-changes-in-a-partial-export" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pinia changes in a partial export&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>Change store</strong> event is a diff against the previous event of the same store. When that previous event is not part of the export (a selection, a filter or a single event), the exported change also carries the full state after it and the old values of the changed fields, so <strong>Before</strong> / <strong>After</strong> stay available in the file.</p><h3 id="files" tabindex="-1">Files <a class="header-anchor" href="#files" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Files&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Export</th><th>File name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Export Logs</strong></td><td><code>logs_&lt;date&gt;_&lt;time&gt;.html</code></td></tr><tr><td>Network pair</td><td><code>log_network_&lt;date&gt;_&lt;time&gt;.html</code></td></tr><tr><td>Other single event</td><td><code>log_&lt;type&gt;_&lt;date&gt;_&lt;time&gt;.html</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Data is masked at capture time, so the files contain the same masked values as the view (see <a href="./settings.html#data-masking">Logs settings</a>).</p><hr><h2 id="html-report" tabindex="-1">HTML report <a class="header-anchor" href="#html-report" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;HTML report&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>HTML</strong> report is one self-contained file: styles, scripts, the application icon and the event data are embedded. It uses a dark theme, does not load external resources and opens offline from disk.</p><h3 id="sidebar" tabindex="-1">Sidebar <a class="header-anchor" href="#sidebar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sidebar&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Report</strong> — <strong>Overview</strong> and <strong>All events</strong> with the total count;</li><li>sections by type with counters: <strong>Network</strong> (<strong>Send request</strong>, <strong>Receive response</strong>), <strong>WebSocket</strong> (<strong>WebSocket open</strong>, <strong>WebSocket sent</strong>, <strong>WebSocket received</strong>, <strong>WebSocket closed</strong>), <strong>Components</strong> (<strong>Change props</strong>, <strong>Emit</strong>), <strong>Pinia</strong> (<strong>Change store</strong>, <strong>Store action</strong>), <strong>Page</strong> (<strong>Navigation</strong>, <strong>JS error</strong>);</li><li><strong>Download Vue Inspector</strong> at the bottom — link to the project page.</li><li><strong>Support Vue Inspector</strong> above it: <strong>Monthly support</strong> opens Boosty, <strong>Donate with USDT</strong> shows the Tron (TRC-20) address, a QR code and <strong>Copy address</strong>.</li></ul><h3 id="overview" tabindex="-1">Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Overview&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Summary cards: <strong>Events</strong>, <strong>Errors</strong>, <strong>Failed requests</strong>, first and last event time, time span; counters by type; export information. <strong>Generator</strong> is <strong>Vue Inspector Extension</strong> or <strong>Vue Inspector Bookmarklet</strong>, matching the mode that created the file.</p><p>The header shows <strong>Vue Inspector</strong> with that mode in a tag, <strong>Logs report</strong> on the next line, and the event count with the export time centered beside those two lines.</p><p><strong>Events</strong>, <strong>Errors</strong> and <strong>Failed requests</strong> are clickable: they open <strong>All events</strong> with the matching filter. The active filter is shown as a chip above the list; clicking the chip removes it.</p><h3 id="event-list-and-details" tabindex="-1">Event list and details <a class="header-anchor" href="#event-list-and-details" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Event list and details&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Search in the top bar; with <strong>in data</strong> enabled the search also looks inside event data.</li><li><strong>All events</strong> has a <strong>Type</strong> dropdown with checkboxes, the same control as on the <strong>Logs</strong> tab. Every type is on by default. <strong>Select all</strong> and <strong>Clear</strong> turn the whole list on or off. With every type off, the list is empty. The filter stays on this page while you search or use the <strong>Errors</strong> / <strong>Failed requests</strong> chip, and does not change the sidebar sections.</li><li>Clicking a row opens details with the same tabs as in the app (<strong>Overview</strong>, <strong>Params</strong>, <strong>Headers</strong>, <strong>Body</strong>, <strong>Changes</strong>, <strong>Before</strong> / <strong>After</strong>, <strong>State</strong>, <strong>Raw</strong>, etc.). Every JSON and text block has the same bar on top: <strong>Find</strong>, the previous / next match arrows, the match count and <strong>Copy</strong>. JSON is indented and colored, including a truncated body. <strong>Find</strong> searches the whole block and highlights the matches; Enter and Shift+Enter move between them, Esc clears the field. <strong>Copy</strong> copies the stored text. While details with such a block are open, Ctrl+F (Cmd+F) moves to its <strong>Find</strong> field in any keyboard layout; a second Ctrl+F opens the browser find. A block larger than <strong>200 KB</strong> fills the details area and keeps only the visible lines in the page, so only <strong>Find</strong> sees all of it. Smaller blocks are on the page in full, so the browser find works on them too.</li><li>A binary response shows the same yellow notice as a truncated body, <em>Binary response received. Body not included in report.</em>, above the type line (<code>image/jpeg (986.2 KB)</code> and <strong>Binary</strong>).</li><li><strong>WebSocket open</strong>, <strong>WebSocket sent</strong>, <strong>WebSocket received</strong> and <strong>WebSocket closed</strong> are a separate <strong>WebSocket</strong> section. A message is pretty-printed JSON when the frame is JSON. <strong>Sent</strong> or <strong>Received</strong> and the size sit above that block, in the same type-and-size line as a binary file. <strong>WebSocket closed</strong> lists each frame the same way. When the tab holds several messages, one <strong>Find</strong> bar sits above them and stays visible while scrolling: it searches all messages together, the count and the arrows go through the matches across the messages, and each message keeps its own <strong>Copy</strong>. <strong>Overview</strong> shows the close code, the reason and <strong>No automatic reconnect</strong> when that was determined.</li><li>For network events, <strong>Request</strong> or <strong>Response</strong> in the details header jumps between the request and its response. A WebSocket frame or the open event uses <strong>Closed</strong>; the close event uses <strong>Opened</strong>.</li><li><strong>Locate</strong> opens <strong>All events</strong> and scrolls so this event is in view, the same way <strong>Request</strong> / <strong>Response</strong> brings its pair into view. If its type was switched off, that type is switched on so the event stays in the list. When the details panel covers the list, <strong>Locate</strong> also closes the panel so the event stays visible in the list.</li><li>The details panel closes with the back arrow on the left (the same control as in the app) or with <strong>Esc</strong>.</li><li><strong>↑ / ↓</strong> move between events.</li><li>The selected section and event are kept in the address (<code>#…</code>), so a link to a specific event can be shared and survives a reload.</li><li>On a narrow screen the section list opens from the menu button. Choosing a section closes it, and a tap outside it closes it. Event rows show a colored bar for the event type.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="see-also" tabindex="-1">See also <a class="header-anchor" href="#see-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See also&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./general.html">Working with logs</a></li><li><a href="./settings.html">Logs settings</a></li><li><a href="/qa-tools/docs/network/export.html">Export collections</a></li></ul>',35)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{c as __pageData,m as default};
